It isn't easy being a god. Take yesterday for example. First there was Zeus's temple-warming party: all those relatives you hadn't seen for millennia; all those offspring you didn't know you had. Of course, brother Zeus was there with his wife Hera - the perfect hosts as ever. Kronos too. Dear old dad, I suppose it was nice to see him again after all this time. Ha, I remember when he first took me to the seaside.
 "Poseidon," he'd called as I paddled in the shallows. "Poseidon, don't you wander out too far now. Stay close to the shore; you know you can't swim."
 How that used to embarrass me! There I am, God of the Sea no less and I couldn't even swim - still can't come to think of it. Have you any idea how difficult it is to look god-like and awe-inspiring clad in a pair of red water-wings?
 Never mind, that's the least of my worries right now. You see, the party turned out to be little more than a pretext designed to lure us deities, en masse, to a meeting. And it worked. There we were, eating and drinking and making merry; everything was just fine. Then, part-way through - I think we were a year or so into the party which would make it thirty AD or thereabouts - Zeus cleared his throat.
 "Er, could I have your attention please?" he said, tapping a wine glass with his fruit knife. "Excuse me. Er, you at the back. Persephone. Be so good as to pipe down for a moment would you? Thank you so much dear.
 "Gentlemen," he began, "our religious monopoly is being eroded! Wherever you look new gods are coming to the fore. Yes, I know, we've seen them come and we've seen them go. But some are here to stay; just look at how we underestimated the Romans! Now I foresee a new threat. Something which threatens irrevocable damage. Something so potentially pervasive that it may eventually usurp us. Christianity, gentlemen, is our enemy; and I for one do not intend to go down without a fight!"
 This was rousing stuff! Everyone who could stand was on their feet, clapping and cheering.
 "I propose," continued Zeus, "that we tighten our slack attitudes, rout our complacent outlooks and rid ourselves of these pot-bellies. Only then will we be fit to take on this challenge with the necessary courage and vitality."
 The cheers were not so loud now. But Zeus had us on the run. He launched into his closing speech. "To this end I intend to set each and every one of you a task to test your mettle." He glanced about the room. "Now, who shall be first? Ah... Yes... Poseidon."
 And that was it. There wasn't even time to get a sick note. Zeus handed me a slip of paper, there was this flash and a clap of thunder. And here I am, at the gateway to The Underworld.

